input.findings:
ROOT CAUSE
==========
The effect symbol's rotation is derived from a "am I on the return leg?" test written as `symbol.__t < 1`. That test is only valid when `roundTrip: true` (where `__t` animates 0 -> 2, and `__t > 1` really is the return leg).

When `roundTrip` is falsy, `_animateSymbol` animates `__t` from 0 to **1** (src/chart/helper/EffectLine.ts:169-172):
```ts
            const animator = symbol.animate('', loop)
                .when(roundTrip ? period * 2 : period, {
                    __t: roundTrip ? 2 : 1
                })
```
zrender's Clip clamps and *always emits a frame at exactly percent === 1* before looping (node_modules/zrender/lib/animation/Clip.js:33-38: `percent = Math.min(percent, 1); ... this.onframe(schedule); if (percent === 1) { if (this.loop) {...} }`). So on the last frame of every loop iteration `symbol.__t === 1`, `symbol.__t < 1` is false, and the tangent is computed from the REVERSED control points -> the symbol snaps 180 degrees for that frame at the end of every loop. Visible as a flicker/backwards flip with directional symbols (arrow, `planePath`), e.g. test/lines-symbol.html:83-89 (plane symbol, `loop` default true, no `roundTrip`).

Position is unaffected: `const t = symbol.__t < 1 ? symbol.__t : 2 - symbol.__t;` yields `t === 1` either way at `__t === 1`. Only the rotation branch is wrong.

FIX STRATEGY
============
Record the current animation mode on the symbol (mirroring the existing `__t` / `__lastT` pattern) and use `symbol.__roundTrip && symbol.__t >= 1` as the "return leg" test. `!(x < 1)` is identically `x >= 1`, so roundTrip behavior is bit-for-bit unchanged; only the non-roundTrip end-of-loop frame changes.
A field on the symbol (rather than `this._roundTrip`) is required because `_roundTrip` is declared `private` (src/chart/helper/EffectLine.ts:50) and `EffectPolyline._updateSymbolPosition` needs it too. (Alternative, if you prefer: change `private _roundTrip` -> `protected _roundTrip` and read `this._roundTrip`; it is assigned on every `_updateEffectAnimation` call so it is always current before any `during` frame fires. The symbol-field version below is preferred - it is immune to update ordering and to the symbol being recreated.)

